## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses practical mastery of LangChain + Autogen to orchestrate real tools under realistic constraints, including error handling and plan adaptation.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain chains and agents
- Autogen planning vs. manual step delineation
- Tool invocation patterns and idempotency
- Robust error handling: retries, fallbacks, timeouts
- Observability and testability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: planner -> tool calls
function planAndRun(query) {
  const plan = autogenPlan(query);
  for (const step of plan.steps) {
    // call corresponding tool
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test behavior under partial tool failures?
- How would you guard against rate limits and stale data?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding cross-language adapter strategies with strict latency and memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT vs full fine-tuning in multi-language QA
- Adapter injection points (attention vs MLP)
- Language tagging vs per-language adapters
- Latency/memory budgeting and rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo adapter injection sketch
function attachAdapters(model, keys) {
  for (const m of model.modules()) {
    if (m instanceof AttentionLayer || m instanceof MLP) {
      m.addAdapter({rank: 8, name: keys.next()});
    }
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift between languages post-deployment?
- What failure modes require bypassing adapters and using base model instead?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate’s ability to design end-to-end evaluation for private, privacy-conscious enterprise assistants, focusing on faithfulness, hallucination, and safety under real-world data/versioning constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding to versioned sources and source IDs
- Hallucination detection in live responses
- Privacy safety: PII/secret leakage checks in output and citations
- Performance metrics under load and latency budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal evaluation script outline (pseudo)
function evaluate(model, corpus) {
  const prompts = loadPrompts();
  return prompts.map(p => {
    const out = model.respond(p);
    const grounded = ground(out, corpus, p);
    return { prompt: p, output: out, grounded };
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect partial grounding failures (e.g., only some claims anchored)?
- How would you integrate this into a CI/CD pipeline with private data access controls?

## Why This Is Asked
To evaluate ability to design end-to-end retrieval systems with multilingual data, latency constraints, and production-grade maintenance. The candidate must articulate chunking, embeddings, indexing, reranking, provenance, and monitoring trade-offs in a realistic enterprise setting.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation
- Embeddings for multilingual data
- Vector databases and indexing strategies (IVF+PQ, HNSW)
- Document chunking and provenance
- Reranking with cross-encoders
- Drifts, updates, caching, latency budgets
- Evaluation metrics: recall@k, QA accuracy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: initial retrieval then rerank
function retrieveAnswer(query, docs){
  const chunks = chunkDocs(docs);
  const emb = embed(query);
  const candidates = vectorDB.search(emb, topK=10);
  const ranked = reranker.rank(query, candidates);
  return formatAnswer(ranked[0]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor embedding drift across languages?
- How would you handle adding a new language with few examples?
- What metrics would you use to balance latency vs. accuracy?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to engineer a practical, scalable evaluation system for real-world multilingual policy assistants operating under complex brand-specific constraints with live documentation dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual policy alignment across distinct brand ecosystems
- Versioned, source-grounded citation verification and tracking
- Cross-brand policy conflict detection and temporal drift analysis
- Lightweight evaluation datasets with granular per-claim scoring mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example citation validation function
function validateCitationCurrentness(claim, policyIndex) {
  const citation = claim?.citation;
  const policy = policyIndex.find(p => p.id === citation?.policyId);
  return policy && policy.version === citation?.version && policy.isActive;
}
```
